        "answer": "Methane reacts with chlorine in the presence of sunlight through a substitution reaction. In this reaction, one or more hydrogen atoms of methane are replaced by chlorine atoms. The reaction occurs in steps. For example, methane reacting with chlorine in the presence of sunlight produces chloromethane and hydrogen chloride. This is a substitution reaction because a chlorine atom (or a group of atoms) replaces a hydrogen atom. Higher homologues of alkanes can form a mixture of products as multiple substitutions can occur.",
